window.docContents[141].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Game/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Game":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh › Game.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 872 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (69)abbrev KeyIddef word256ToBytesdef word128ToBytesdef sampleBytes32def sampleScalar32def sampleCoins32def sampleRandom64def sampleDRKey96def sampleMessageKeys80def sampleKemKeyGenstructure Setupdef N_spk_totdef N_opk_totdef N_no_opk_totdef N_recv_totdef N_send_opk_totdef N_send_no_opk_totdef N_send_totdef N_sess_totdef N_dhinductive SessionIdstructure PrincipalEntrystructure SpkBundleEntrystructure OpkEntrydef findHonestPrincipaldef resolveSigPkdef isHonestUncorruptedPeerdef checkBundleProvenancedef isAdvRegistereddef isGameIdKeydef isGameSpkdef isGameOpkdef koskBundleOkdef koskSenderOkdef registerAdvKeydef evalHkdfOrRodef checkAndMarkKyberBaseKeySeenstructure KeyIndInitiatorEntrystructure KeyIndRespEntrydef keyIndPartnerdef isSpkRevealeddef keyIndInitCorruptionClausedef isKeyIndInitStillFreshdef isKeyIndRespStillFreshinductive KeyIndQueryinductive KeyIndResponsedef keyIndSpecstructure KeyIndAdversarystructure KeyIndStatedef KeyIndState.emptydef isKeyIndSessionTestabledef recordKeyIndRespdef keyIndPartnerRandKey?def runKeyIndInitiatedef pqxdhKeyIndImpldef isKeyIndRoQueryinstance L794def isKeyIndSendQueryinstance L804def isKeyIndRecvQueryinstance L814def isKeyIndOnlineQueryinstance L826structure QueryBudgetdef isGameQuerydef isGameQueryOfKindclass HasQueryBoundinstance L859def BoundedAdv\n\n\n\nMulti-session key-indistinguishability game infrastructure for PQXDHTrusted: the statement. Defines the multi-session key-indistinguishability (KeyInd) oracle types and handler (KeyIndQuery, KeyIndResponse, KeyIndState, pqxdhKeyIndImpl), freshness predicates (isKeyIndInitStillFresh, isKeyIndRespStillFresh), replication bounds (Setup), and query budgets (QueryBudget) used by Security/Headline.lean, Security/HeadlineDelta.lean, and Assumptions/{KeyInd1KeyReal,KeyInd1KeyRo}.lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Honest parties execute the PQXDH handshake model of LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalPqxdh/Model.lean (Model.acceptKeys, buildSecrets) over abstract Primitives while the adversary controls the network, schedules sessions, corrupts identity keys, and reveals signed pre-key secrets. Game structure and freshness clauses follow the CryptoVerif model PQXDH.m4.ocv of BJKS24 (Bhargavan, Jacomme, Kiefer, Schmidt, USENIX Security 2024; see Computational/docs/README.md).Key modelling choices (Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md):* One-key identities. The headline experiments (KeyInd1KeyReal.lean, KeyInd1KeyRo.lean) override .initPrin to derive both X25519 (secIKA0) and XEdDSA (secIKAsign) from a single 32-byte scalar; PrincipalEntry retains separate fields so the standalone two-key game can share the remaining handlers.* Handshake KDF (DEV-PQ14). useRo = true models HkdfRO as a lazy caching random oracle; useRo = false evaluates P.hkdfDerive96.* Known-secret-key (KOSK) registration (DEV-PQ15). Adversarial X25519 identity, signed, and one-time pre-keys supplied to honest sessions must be registered with their secret scalar via registerKey (or be game-generated keys of the same role), weakening the model by restricting the adversary.* Canonical ephemeral keys (DEV-PQ16). Responders reject non-canonical X25519 ephemeral keys ekAPk.\n\n\n\nabbrev KeyIdL53 ↗#\n\n\n\nUniform byte sampling\n\ndef word256ToBytesL59–61 ↗#Little-endian 32-byte encoding of w.\n\ndef word128ToBytesL63–65 ↗#Little-endian 16-byte encoding of w.\n\ndef sampleBytes32L67–68 ↗#\n\ndef sampleScalar32L70–71 ↗#\n\ndef sampleCoins32L73–74 ↗#\n\ndef sampleRandom64L76–79 ↗#\n\ndef sampleDRKey96L81–85 ↗#\n\ndef sampleMessageKeys80L87–91 ↗#\n\ndef sampleKemKeyGenL93–95 ↗#\n\nReplication bounds and session identifiers\n\nstructure SetupL99–108 ↗#Multi-user, multi-session replication bounds on principals, pre-key bundles, one-time pre-keys, and sessions (PQXDH.m4.ocv).\n\n\n\ndef N_spk_totL112 ↗#\n\ndef N_opk_totL114 ↗#\n\ndef N_no_opk_totL116 ↗#\n\ndef N_recv_totL118 ↗#\n\ndef N_send_opk_totL120 ↗#\n\ndef N_send_no_opk_totL122 ↗#\n\ndef N_send_totL124 ↗#\n\ndef N_sess_totL126 ↗#\n\ndef N_dhL128–129 ↗#Total honestly generated X25519 keypairs across the game.\n\n\n\ninductive SessionIdL133–140 ↗#Session slot identifier for initiator (sendOPK, sendNoOPK) and responder (recvOPK, recvNoOPK) sessions.\n\nPrincipal, bundle, and pre-key state\n\nstructure PrincipalEntryL144–153 ↗#State of an initialized honest principal u : Fin S.N_id. The one-key headline games set secIKAsign := secIKA0 and ikSignPk := ikAPk from a single scalar; the fields remain separate so the standalone two-key game can share pqxdhKeyIndImpl.\n\nstructure SpkBundleEntryL155–164 ↗#Honestly generated signed EC pre-key and Kyber-1024 pre-key bundle (u, j).\n\nstructure OpkEntryL166–171 ↗#Honestly generated one-time EC pre-key (u, j, o).\n\ndef findHonestPrincipalL173–176 ↗#First honest principal whose ikAPk matches ikB modulo the cofactor projection pow8.\n\ndef resolveSigPkL178–183 ↗#XEdDSA verification key for ikB: entry.ikSignPk if ikB belongs to an honest principal, and ikB otherwise.\n\ndef isHonestUncorruptedPeerL185–190 ↗#Index of the honest principal matching ikB if currently uncorrupted, or none.\n\ndef checkBundleProvenanceL192–212 ↗#Whether (spkB, opkB?, pqspkB) in bundle were honestly generated by the principal owning bundle.ikB (with EC keys compared modulo pow8).\n\nKnown-secret-key (KOSK) registration (DEV-PQ15)Every X25519 public key supplied to an honest session in an identity, signed-pre-key, or one-time-pre-key position must either be a game-generated key of the same role or be registered with its 32-byte secret scalar via registerKey sk (all comparisons modulo pow8; ekAPk and pqspkB are unrestricted). Without KOSK, adversarial keys with unknown discrete logs induce a 4-point DH relation on DH1 that a 3-point GapDH oracle cannot test. Registration weakens the model by disallowing unregistered unknown-secret public keys, while remaining pure bookkeeping that does not mark keys honest or affect freshness or partnering.\n\ndef isAdvRegisteredL224–225 ↗#\n\ndef isGameIdKeyL227–228 ↗#\n\ndef isGameSpkL230–232 ↗#\n\ndef isGameOpkL234–236 ↗#\n\ndef koskBundleOkL238–248 ↗#Role-strict KOSK check on an initiator's peer bundle (ikB, spkB, and opkB? modulo pow8).\n\ndef koskSenderOkL250–253 ↗#Role-strict KOSK check on a responder's claimed sender identity key ikAPk.\n\ndef registerAdvKeyL255–261 ↗#Register a 32-byte adversarial secret scalar sk as (P.x25519Base sk, (sk, pk) :: advKeys).\n\ndef evalHkdfOrRoL263–274 ↗#Evaluate the 96-byte handshake KDF via the lazy caching random oracle roCache when useRo = true (DEV-PQ14) or via P.hkdfDerive96 when useRo = false.\n\ndef checkAndMarkKyberBaseKeySeenL276–285 ↗#Insert (k, pqspkId, spkId, ekAPk) into Bob's seen-base-key set if absent; the Bool result is true iff the tuple is fresh.\n\nKey-indistinguishability game (KeyInd)\n\nstructure KeyIndInitiatorEntryL289–307 ↗#Initiator session record in KeyInd.\n\nstructure KeyIndRespEntryL309–322 ↗#Responder session record in KeyInd, created on successful acceptOPK or acceptNoOPK.\n\ndef keyIndPartnerL324–335 ↗#Partnering between initiator session s and responder session r: agreement on all KDF-determining keys (ikAPk, peerIkPk/respIkPk, spkPk, and opkPk? modulo pow8, plus byte-exact ekAPk, pqspkPk, and kemCt). Malleable header key IDs are ignored.\n\ndef isSpkRevealedL337–341 ↗#Whether pk matches (modulo pow8) a game-generated SPK whose secret was revealed via revealSpkSk.\n\ndef keyIndInitCorruptionClauseL343–358 ↗#Post-game key-compromise freshness clause for an initiator session s that was fresh at initiation (s.fresh = true):* Classical (useRo = true): either the peer identity key remains uncorrupted, an unrevealed honest SPK was used, or an honest OPK was used (pqspkHonest is excluded because the classical bound does not assume KEM IND-CCA).* Post-quantum HNDL (useRo = false, where .revealDhSecrets exposes X25519 scalars): s.pqspkHonest.\n\ndef isKeyIndInitStillFreshL360–368 ↗#End-of-game freshness of a tested initiator session s: keyIndInitCorruptionClause holds, s is unrevealed, and every partnered responder session is unrevealed with senderHonest = true (dishonest-sender accepts return .drKeyResp immediately).\n\ndef isKeyIndRespStillFreshL370–385 ↗#End-of-game freshness of a tested responder session r: r is unrevealed with r.senderHonest = true, and if r has any partnered initiator session then every partner is unrevealed and satisfies keyIndInitCorruptionClause; otherwise (unpartnered) useRo = true, the claimed sender remains uncorrupted, and r.spkPk has not been revealed via revealSpkSk.\n\ninductive KeyIndQueryL387–405 ↗#Oracle queries of the KeyInd game. Includes KOSK registration (registerKey, DEV-PQ15) and signed-pre-key secret reveal (revealSpkSk, used when composing with the Double Ratchet SM game where Bob's SPK serves as his initial ratchet key).\n\ninductive KeyIndResponseL407–420 ↗#Oracle responses of the KeyInd game.\n\ndef keyIndSpecL422–423 ↗#\n\nstructure KeyIndAdversaryL425–427 ↗#Adversary in the KeyInd game.\n\nstructure KeyIndStateL429–441 ↗#Mutable game state for KeyInd.\n\ndef KeyIndState.emptyL443–454 ↗#\n\ndef isKeyIndSessionTestableL456–465 ↗#Whether testSession sid is permitted: sid must be unrevealed, untested, and initiated toward an uncorrupted honest peer (e.fresh, initiator) or accepted from an honest claimed sender (r.senderHonest, responder).\n\ndef recordKeyIndRespL467–475 ↗#Record an accepted responder session; reuses a partnered initiator's drKeyRand when one exists so partnered sessions share one random-world key, and samples a fresh key otherwise.\n\ndef keyIndPartnerRandKey?L477–480 ↗#Random-world key of an already recorded responder session partnered with s, if any.\n\ndef runKeyIndInitiateL482–545 ↗#Execute an initiator handshake query (initiateOPK or initiateNoOPK); reuses any already-recorded partnered responder's drKeyRand so accept-before-initiate partners remain consistent in the random world.\n\ndef pqxdhKeyIndImplL547–786 ↗#Shared KeyInd oracle handler (useRo = true for HkdfRO, useRo = false for concrete P.hkdfDerive96). The one-key headline handlers override .initPrin to derive both identity keys from a single scalar and delegate all other queries here.\n\nQuery-budget predicates\n\ndef isKeyIndRoQueryL790–792 ↗#\n\ninstanceL794–797 ↗#\n\ndef isKeyIndSendQueryL799–802 ↗#\n\ninstanceL804–807 ↗#\n\ndef isKeyIndRecvQueryL809–812 ↗#\n\ninstanceL814–817 ↗#\n\ndef isKeyIndOnlineQueryL819–824 ↗#Online (non-random-oracle) game queries in KeyIndQuery S: every .inr q except .roQuery _.\n\ninstanceL826–829 ↗#\n\nstructure QueryBudgetL831–839 ↗#Explicit oracle-query budget for a game adversary: q_ro HkdfRO queries, q_roRoot RootKdfRO queries, q_total total game-oracle queries (isGameQuery), and q_on online non-RO queries (isKeyIndOnlineQuery). Passed explicitly to reductions so they never inspect A.\n\ndef isGameQueryL841–844 ↗#True on game-oracle queries (.inr _) and false on uniform random sampling (.inl _).\n\ndef isGameQueryOfKindL846–850 ↗#True on game-oracle queries .inr t of kind i (kind t = i) and false on uniform random sampling (.inl _); used to state per-kind query bounds (CostBoundedByKind).\n\nclass HasQueryBoundL852–855 ↗#Typeclass asserting that an adversary A respects a QueryBudget together with the session-slot bounds from Setup.\n\n\n\ninstanceL859–865 ↗#\n\ndef BoundedAdvL867–873 ↗#Adversary paired with a proof that it respects query budget b. A has unrestricted computation steps; efficiency enters via explicit step-cost bounds on the reductions.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Game.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Game/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Game"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSessionApi/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSessionApi":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet › LibsignalSessionApi.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 75 of 450 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (8)structure PendingPreKeystructure PendingKyberPreKeystructure ProtoPreKeySignalMessagedef varintdef keydef uint32Fielddef bytesFielddef ProtoPreKeySignalMessage.encode\n\nlibsignal's public session API over abstract storesTrusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use only key, PreKeySignalMessage, new, ProtoPreKeySignalMessage, encode, PendingPreKey, PendingKyberPreKey, KyberPayload, varint, uint32Field, bytesField from this file (the pre-key message types and the Prost field encoders): read them. Everything else here (the session API, its errors and store calls) and every proof is checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Models the public entry points messageEncrypt (session_management.rs:43–151) and messageDecryptSignal (session_management.rs:297–354) over the core session model of LibsignalSession.lean:* Time and staleness: SystemTime (checkedAddSecs, lt) and the 30-day unacknowledged session limit maxUnacknowledgedSessionAge.* Session records and PreKeySignalMessage: PendingPreKey, PendingKyberPreKey, Session, SessionRecord, KyberPayload, ProtoPreKeySignalMessage, PreKeySignalMessage, CiphertextMessage, and concrete Prost wire encoders (varint, key, uint32Field, bytesField).* Stores and errors: Direction, IdentityChange, StoreAnswers, StoreCall, ApiError, Api, storeCall, liftProtocol, and execOk. Each entry point invokes at most the four store methods (loadSession, isTrustedIdentity, saveIdentity, storeSession) in fixed order, taking their answers via StoreAnswers and emitting the executed StoreCall trace.* Scope and panics: single current session (previous_sessions = []), log::max_level() = Off, distinct local and remote identity keys (self_session = false), canonical 33-byte stored keys, and ApiError.panic modeling panic = \"abort\" non-termination (SystemTime overflow or session-model u32::MAX overflow).\n\n\n\n5 declarations not shown (lines 53–88): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime.tvNsec, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime.tvSec, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime.unixEpoch, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime.checkedAddSecs, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SystemTime.lt, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.maxUnacknowledgedSessionAge\n\nThe session record (state/session.rs, proto/storage.proto)\n\nstructure PendingPreKeyL92–104 ↗#session_structure::PendingPreKey (proto/storage.proto:35–40), as unacknowledged_pre_key_message_items reads it (state/session.rs:549–564).\n\nstructure PendingKyberPreKeyL106–114 ↗#session_structure::PendingKyberPreKey (proto/storage.proto:42–45): the Kyber pre-key id (KyberPreKeyId) and the KEM (key encapsulation mechanism) ciphertext of an unacknowledged PQXDH session.\n\n4 declarations not shown (lines 116–156): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.pendingPreKey, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.localRegistrationId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.pendingKyberPreKey, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.aliceBaseKey, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.state, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.remoteRegistrationId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Session.Wf, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SessionRecord.currentSession, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SessionRecord, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.KyberPayload, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.KyberPayload.preKeyId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.KyberPayload.ciphertext\n\nstructure ProtoPreKeySignalMessageL158–177 ↗#prost's proto::wire::PreKeySignalMessage (proto/wire.proto:19–28, proto2: every field is optional).\n\n\n\ndef varintL184–189 ↗#encode_varint (prost/src/encoding/varint.rs:11–22): base-128 (LEB128), 7 bits per byte, least significant group first, the high bit set on every byte but the last.\n\ndef keyL191–192 ↗#encode_key (prost/src/encoding.rs:106–110): the varint of tag << 3 | wire_type.\n\ndef uint32FieldL194–199 ↗#An optional uint32 field (uint32::encode: the varint! macro, prost/src/encoding.rs:263–266, at :356): nothing for None; else key (wire type 0, varint) and the value as a varint.\n\ndef bytesFieldL201–205 ↗#An optional bytes field (bytes::encode, prost/src/encoding.rs:691–695): nothing for None; else key (wire type 2, length-delimited), the length as a varint, the bytes.\n\n\n\ndef ProtoPreKeySignalMessage.encodeL209–222 ↗#prost's encoder for proto::wire::PreKeySignalMessage (Message::encode, infallible into a Vec: expect(\"can always append to a Vec\")): the fields in ascending tag order (prost-derive sorts the fields by tag, prost-derive/src/lib.rs:91), each Some field emitted, each None omitted. Concrete, unlike the session model's abstract SignalMessage codec; checked byte-exactly by the KATs (known-answer tests).\n\n17 declarations not shown (lines 224–447): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.preKeyId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.message, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.identityKey, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.registrationId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.kyberPayload, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.messageVersion, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.baseKey, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.signedPreKeyId, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.serialized, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.PreKeySignalMessage.new, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.CiphertextMessage.signalMessage, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.CiphertextMessage, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.CiphertextMessage.preKeySignalMessage, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Direction.sending, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Direction, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Direction.receiving, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.IdentityChange.replacedExisting, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.IdentityChange, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.IdentityChange.newOrUnchanged, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreAnswers.isTrustedIdentity, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreAnswers, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreAnswers.loadSession, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreAnswers.storeSession, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreAnswers.saveIdentity, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreCall.saveIdentity, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreCall.isTrustedIdentity, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreCall.loadSession, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreCall, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.StoreCall.storeSession, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.protocol, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.sessionNotFound, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.store, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.panic, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.untrustedIdentity, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Api, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.Api.run, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.storeCall, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.ApiError.ofProtocol, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.liftProtocol, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.execOk, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.messageEncrypt, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.SessionRecord.tryDecryptFromRecord, CryptoGames.LibsignalSessionApi.messageDecryptSignal\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet/LibsignalSessionApi.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSessionApi/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSessionApi"}});